window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][35] = "<h5>IQ-C19-07 · Hard · strategy · ST</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Define the metrics you would use to run a developer platform for a cloud API, including how you would tell early that something is going wrong.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Number of developers and API calls.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Defines a primary metric and metrics for acquisition, activation, usage, quality and revenue.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses time to first call and production share, adds ecosystem health (concentration, churn), builds leading indicators and guardrails, and reads trends by cohort.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'd start from the platform's core value: developers building products that work and keep working. A volume metric such as API calls can rise while developer satisfaction and ecosystem health fall, so I'd use a primary metric and guardrails.</p>\n<p><em>Primary metric.</em> Developers whose integrations are live in production and active over the last 30 days (production-active developers). It reflects success, not interest.</p>\n<p><em>The funnel (FW-C19-04).</em> Sign-ups; the share who get credentials; the share who make a first successful call, with the median time to it; the share who reach production. Each stage is a place to look for friction; the first call time is the leading indicator of adoption.</p>\n<p><em>Usage and value.</em> API calls per production-active developer; the number of distinct endpoints used (breadth); revenue per developer, and the share of developers growing their usage.</p>\n<p><em>Quality and trust.</em> API availability and latency at the 95th and 99th percentiles; error rate for correct requests; support tickets per 1,000 developers and time to resolve; documentation feedback; the number of breaking-change incidents.</p>\n<p><em>Ecosystem health.</em> Concentration: the share of API volume or revenue from the top 10 developers; developer churn (production-active developers lost per month) by cohort; multi-homing (developers also using rivals) from surveys; the share of new developers coming from referrals.</p>\n<p><em>Early warnings.</em> (1) Time to first call lengthening for new cohorts, before sign-ups fall. (2) The production share of the latest sign-up cohort falling relative to earlier cohorts. (3) Rising error rates on a version before its deprecation. (4) Support ticket growth faster than developer growth. (5) Rising concentration, meaning growth is coming from a few large accounts. Read them by cohort, since averages blend old developers who are fine with new ones who are struggling (C13, C05).</p>\n<p><em>Guardrails.</em> No improvement in sign-ups counts if the production share falls; no change counts that raises the error rate.</p>\n<p><em>How I'd act.</em> A weekly dashboard by side and cohort, a threshold on each early warning with an owner, and a monthly review of developer interviews alongside the numbers. What would change the set: a platform with a very different developer type (enterprise integrators versus hobbyists) would need separate funnels.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What leading indicators show that something is going wrong? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Rising time to first successful call; a falling share of new integrations that reach production within 30 days; rising error rates in new integrations; a falling documentation satisfaction score; growing concentration of traffic in a few developers; and rising support contacts per 1,000 developers. Any one can be noise, so I would watch the set and set alerts, for example if the production share falls by five points two months in a row. These move before API call volume does, which is why call volume alone is a poor early warning.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you read the trends by cohort? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By sign-up cohort. Compare the share of each quarter's new developers who are production-active at 90 days: if the first-quarter cohort reached 35% and the third-quarter cohort reached 22%, onboarding has regressed even if total active developers are growing because older cohorts are loyal. I would split by developer type (hobbyist, startup, enterprise) and by region, then look for the step where the new cohort stalls, whether it is the first call, the first deployment or billing set-up. Cohorts show the health of the pipeline, which totals hide.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c19-12-exercises\"></span>\n<h3>12. Exercises</h3>\n<p>Ten exercises. Each has a model solution and an alternative. Figures are assumptions unless marked, and the arithmetic was computed.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c19-01\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C19-01 · Classify the platform</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Classify each as a two-sided market, a developer platform, a hybrid, or not a platform, with one reason: (a) a ride-hailing app; (b) a cloud computing service; (c) a mobile app store; (d) a single-player puzzle game with millions of users; (e) a freelance job board; (f) a video streaming service with licensed content.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A warm-up.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Four minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C19-02.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) Two-sided market: riders and drivers need each other. (b) Developer platform: others build on it. (c) Hybrid: developers build and users buy. (d) Not a platform: no outside builders or interaction among users. (e) Two-sided market: employers and candidates. (f) Mostly not a platform: users do not transact with one another or build on it, though it may have partner tools; check before applying the lens.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Note that (a) also has developer features if it exposes an API for partners.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> The label matters because it brings the right problems.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c19-02\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C19-02 · Map the sides</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Build a platform map for a freight marketplace connecting shippers and trucking companies: what each gives and gets, their alternatives and fears, and the first constraint.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A new platform.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None; reason.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C19-01.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> <em>Shippers</em> give loads and payment; get reliable capacity, price transparency, tracking; alternatives: brokers, existing carriers; fears: late or damaged freight, unreliable carriers. <em>Carriers</em> give trucks and driving; get full trucks (less empty running), faster payment; alternatives: brokers, regular customers; fears: low rates, slow payment, bad loads. <em>The platform</em> gives matching, payment, trust (verification, insurance), tracking. The value loop: more loads attract carriers, which lowers prices and raises reliability, which attracts shippers. The first constraint: lane density (loads and trucks on the same routes at the same time), so start on a few busy lanes, not nationally. Risks: disintermediation after the first load, trust and safety, and slow payment.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a third side, drivers, if they are distinct from carriers.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Starting on busy lanes is slow to expand and quick to prove.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c19-03\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C19-03 · How many suppliers for 90%?</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Each supplier fulfills a request with probability 2%. How many suppliers does a market need for an 85% match rate? If each supplier's chance is 8%, how many for 95%?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Cold-start planning.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> P(match) = 1 − (1 − p)ᴺ.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C19-03.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> For p = 0.02 and 85%: N = ln(0.15) ÷ ln(0.98) = 93.9, so 94 suppliers. For p = 0.08 and 95%: N = ln(0.05) ÷ ln(0.92) = 35.9, so 36. A smaller per-supplier chance requires far more suppliers: a quarter of the chance needs about 2.6 times the suppliers for a lower target. The lesson: raising each supplier's chance of fulfilling (by narrowing the offering, improving matching or availability) can reduce the density needed more than recruiting would.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Narrow the category so p rises from 2% to 5%: the need drops to ln(0.15) ÷ ln(0.95) = 37 suppliers.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Narrowing reduces the addressable market and makes the first market work.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c19-04\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C19-04 · The developer funnel</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> 8,000 developers sign up. 75% get credentials, 65% of those make a first successful call, 60% of those reach production. Compute the end-to-end share and count. If the steps are improved to 80%, 75% and 65%, what is the new share and count?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A developer platform review.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C19-04.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> 8,000 × 0.75 = 6,000; × 0.65 = 3,900; × 0.60 = 2,340: 29.25%. Improved: 8,000 × 0.80 × 0.75 × 0.65 = 3,120: 39%. The weakest step in relative terms is the first call (65%); raising it from 65% to 75% adds 10 points of a base of 6,000, or 600 developers at that stage, which flows to 360 in production (at 60%). Look at error messages and the first example.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Improve the last step first (production), since a developer who is already integrated is closest to value.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> The step with the biggest drop is not always the cheapest to fix; check both.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c19-05\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C19-05 · A take-rate model</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> At a 25% take rate, transaction value is 100; at 18%, 110; at 12%, 122. Operating cost is 7% of transaction value. Compute revenue and contribution at each. What would justify the 12% rate?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A pricing review.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Worked example 3.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> 25%: revenue 25.0, cost 7.0, contribution 18.0. 18%: revenue 19.8, cost 7.7, contribution 12.1. 12%: revenue 14.64, cost 8.54, contribution 6.1. Contribution falls by two thirds from 25% to 12%. The lower rate is justified only if it buys something else: faster ecosystem growth that raises the value of the platform's other products, a defense against a rival with a lower rate, regulation, or developer goodwill that has measurable effects on retention. Quantify that benefit and compare it with the lost contribution of about 12 per 100.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Tier the rate: 25% above a threshold of sales, 12% for small participants.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A cut cannot be reversed easily; raise rates rarely and with notice.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c19-06\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C19-06 · Reviewer capacity</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> An app store receives 100,000 submissions a week. 70% are low-risk and automated; 25% are medium risk at 0.5 reviewer-hours each; 5% are high risk at 3 hours each. Reviewers work 35 productive hours a week. How many reviewers are needed? What if automation moves 10 points of submissions from medium to low?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Staffing a review policy.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> IQ-C19-05.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Medium: 25,000 × 0.5 = 12,500 hours. High: 5,000 × 3 = 15,000 hours. Total 27,500 hours; ÷ 35 = 786 reviewers. If 10 points of submissions (10,000) move from medium to low: medium 15,000 × 0.5 = 7,500 hours; total 22,500 hours; ÷ 35 = 643 reviewers, a saving of 143 reviewers. The high-risk tier is more than half the effort for 5% of submissions, which is where specialist skill and good triage pay most.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Reduce the high-risk time with better tooling and checklists.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Moving submissions to the low tier saves cost and raises the risk that something harmful passes; monitor the harmful-approval rate.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c19-07\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C19-07 · Concentration risk</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> The top 10 developers produce 40% of a platform's revenue, and the largest produces 12%. What are the risks, what indicators would you watch, and what would you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> An ecosystem risk review.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Six minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C19-06.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Risks: the platform depends on a few developers, who have leverage over rates and roadmap, and whose departure (or a competing offer) would cut revenue and may pull users away. If the largest leaves, 12% of revenue is at risk. Indicators: the share of revenue from the top 1 and top 10 over time; the top developers' use of rival platforms (multi-homing); their growth versus the rest; contract and renewal dates; sentiment in meetings. Actions: grow the number of mid-sized developers (support, programs), offer volume tiers open to all, keep a regular, structured relationship with top developers without special deals that hurt trust, and track whether the top-10 share falls. Set an alert if the top-10 share rises above a threshold.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Diversify with a program to help new developers reach a first meaningful revenue level.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Heavy investment in the top accounts increases dependence; balance with the broader base.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c19-08\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C19-08 · Disintermediation arithmetic</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A marketplace has 50,000 first jobs a month; 35% of pairs would rebook (17,500); 40% of those leak off-platform (7,000). The take rate is 12% on a $50 job ($6). Compute monthly revenue lost to leakage. Test two interventions: (a) halve the rate on repeat jobs to 6%, cutting leakage to 15%; (b) add a guarantee costing $1 per job that cuts leakage to 20%.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A marketplace fighting leakage.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Seven minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> IQ-C19-06.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Today: on-platform repeat jobs 17,500 − 7,000 = 10,500 × $6 = $63,000; leakage loses 7,000 × $6 = $42,000. (a) Leakage 15% leaves 17,500 × 0.85 = 14,875 on-platform × $3 = $44,625: less than $63,000; the discount does not pay. To match $63,000 at $3 would need 21,000 jobs, more than the 17,500 that exist. (b) Leakage 20% leaves 14,000 on-platform × ($6 − $1) = $70,000: more than $63,000. So the guarantee pays and the discount does not. The principle: reduce leakage by adding continuing value, not by cutting the price of the introduction.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Combine a small guarantee with recurring-booking tools.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A guarantee has a cost and a risk of claims; price it from claim rates.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c19-09\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C19-09 · Platform metrics for a ride-hailing service</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> List a primary metric and metrics for supply, demand, interaction, quality, economics and ecosystem health for a ride-hailing platform, and two early warnings.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> A health dashboard.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Seven minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> FW-C19-07.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> <em>Primary:</em> completed trips per ride request. <em>Supply:</em> active drivers, hours online, new drivers per week. <em>Demand:</em> active riders, requests, new riders. <em>Interaction:</em> request fill rate, time to pickup, cancellation rate. <em>Quality:</em> ratings, safety incidents per 100,000 trips, disputes.</p>\n<p><em>Economics:</em> gross bookings, take rate, contribution per trip, driver earnings per hour. <em>Ecosystem health:</em> share of trips by the top drivers; driver churn by cohort; multi-homing (drivers on rival apps). <em>Early warnings:</em> rising pickup times with flat driver counts (supply thinning where demand is); falling driver earnings per hour (a leading indicator of driver churn). Guardrails: safety and driver earnings must not be sacrificed for rider growth.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Add a measure of surge frequency as an indicator of imbalance.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Many metrics invite confusion; keep one primary and a few guardrails.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c19-10\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C19-10 · A timed full answer</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Give a 25-minute answer to: &quot;A car maker wants to open its in-car screen to third-party apps. Design the platform: sides, cold start, review, revenue and risks.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Self-scored; record yourself. State assumptions aloud.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None. Label what you assume.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Use the 25-minute budget in section 4.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> The platform answer structure, scored against the six behaviors in section 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> An outline of a strong answer. Sides: drivers (want useful, safe apps), developers (want reach, tools and revenue), the car maker (brand, safety, revenue), and regulators (driver distraction). Special constraint: safety in a moving vehicle: apps must limit distraction, so the review is stricter than on a phone, and certain app types (video while driving) are blocked. Cold start: a small set of launch partners in the highest-value categories (navigation, music, charging, parking), a developer kit with a simulator, and single-player value from the car maker's own apps; developer incentives. Review: tiered, with distraction testing and a driving-safety guideline; fast updates for low-risk apps.</p>\n<p>Revenue: a lower take rate initially to attract developers, rising with the platform's reach; subscriptions bundled with the car; data rules (privacy). Risks: safety incidents; fragmentation across car models; the phone's dominance (drivers project their phones), which the car maker must either embrace or compete with; regulatory attention; long vehicle lifecycles (software must be updatable). Metrics: production-active developers, trips with apps used, distraction incidents, developer time to first app. Sequence: pilot with a few models and partners.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Choose to support phone projection first and add native apps later, because drivers already use their phones.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A closed approach protects safety and limits developers; an open one invites innovation and risk.</p>\n<span id=\"c19-13-practice-protocol\"></span>\n<h3>13. Practice protocol</h3>\n<p><strong>Timed drills (three a week for three weeks).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Week 1: sides and the cold start.</em> Use IQ-C19-01, IQ-C19-02, EX-C19-01, EX-C19-02 and EX-C19-03. Draw the map, compute the density needed and say the narrow-launch plan aloud.</li>\n<li><em>Week 2: developers and economics.</em> Use IQ-C19-03, IQ-C19-04, EX-C19-04, EX-C19-05 and EX-C19-08. Compute funnels, take-rate responses and leakage arithmetic by hand.</li>\n<li><em>Week 3: governance, risk and metrics.</em> Use IQ-C19-05, IQ-C19-06, IQ-C19-07, EX-C19-06, EX-C19-07, EX-C19-09 and EX-C19-10. Design a review policy with capacity numbers, scan the risks, and run a full 25-minute answer with a partner who changes the stage midway.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>With a partner.</strong> Ask your partner to say &quot;It has network effects, so it'll grow&quot; and &quot;Why not just cut the fee?&quot; at random. Practice naming the mechanism and its limits, and running the arithmetic.</p>\n<p><strong>Study a real platform.</strong> Read the public rules and fee schedule of one platform you use, map the sides, and list two governance tradeoffs you can see in the document.</p>\n<p><strong>Feedback checklist (yes, partly, no).</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Did I map every side's gives, gets, alternatives and fears?</li>\n<li>Did I classify the platform and name its hard problems?</li>\n<li>Did I treat network effects as a mechanism with limits, and compute density for a cold start?</li>\n<li>Did I treat the API as a product and measure the first mile?</li>\n<li>Did I set governance as a three-way tradeoff and model the take rate with a cost floor?</li>\n<li>Did I scan dependency, concentration, disintermediation, conflict and regulation?</li>\n<li>Did I choose a primary metric tied to successful transactions or production use, with guardrails?</li>\n<li>Did I say what would change my recommendation?</li>\n<li>Did I avoid the label &quot;network effects&quot; without a mechanism?</li>\n<li>Did I finish with the two-sentence close from section 4?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer journal.</strong> For each drill record the date, the platform, the sides, the main tradeoff, the key number, your score out of ten and the step you rushed.</p>\n<span id=\"c19-14-implementation-playbook\"></span>\n<h3>14. Implementation playbook</h3>\n<p>Apply the chapter on real work over the next three weeks. Each deliverable doubles as an interview story. If you do not work on a platform, apply it to your product's relationships with partners, integrations or an internal platform you use.</p>\n<p><strong>Days 1 to 3.</strong> Map your product's sides (FW-C19-01): who gives and gets what, their alternatives and fears. If the map is one-sided, note it and ask a partner or user about the other side.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Find the first-mile funnel for anyone who builds on or integrates with your product (an API, a partner program, an internal tool). Measure or estimate the time to first success and the share who reach production or adoption.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Compute the density or coverage your product needs in a market or segment for matching or fulfillment to work, using the formula, with the best available estimate of p.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Write down the governance rules of your product's ecosystem (quality bar, fees, review) and the three-way tradeoff each makes. Ask a participant what they find unfair or opaque.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Run the risk scan (FW-C19-06): concentration, dependency, leakage, conflict with participants. Pick one indicator to track.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 3.</strong> Define the platform health metrics for your area: a primary metric and guardrails across sides, and one early warning with an owner.</p>\n<p><strong>Deliverables that become stories:</strong> the platform map (a time you saw a problem from the other side), the first-mile funnel (a time you improved a developer or partner experience), and the density or leakage analysis (a time you used a simple model to decide where to focus). Keep the numbers real, and keep what you did separate from what the team did.</p>\n<span id=\"c19-15-key-takeaways-and-further-reading\"></span>\n<h3>15. Key takeaways and further reading</h3>\n<span id=\"c19-key-takeaways\"></span>\n<h4>Key takeaways</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>A platform serves more than one group whose value depends on each other; map each side's gives, gets, alternatives and fears, and classify the platform (two-sided market, developer platform or both) to know which problems it faces.</li>\n<li>Network effects are mechanisms with limits (local, saturating, undermined by multi-homing); for a cold start, concentrate supply where density is reachable, using the matching probability, and measure liquidity, not sign-ups.</li>\n<li>Treat an API as a product: measure the first mile (time to first successful call, share reaching production), protect stability with versioning and deprecation, and read developer experience as the adoption lever.</li>\n<li>Governance is a three-way tradeoff among speed, safety and trust, plus openness and price; model the take rate against participation and the cost floor, tier review by risk, and explain and allow appeals.</li>\n<li>Scan for ecosystem risk (dependency, concentration, disintermediation, conflict with participants, regulation), fight leakage with continuing value and not only price, and run the platform on a primary metric tied to successful transactions with guardrails across sides.</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c19-further-reading\"></span>\n<h4>Further reading</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Geoffrey G. Parker, Marshall W. Van Alstyne and Sangeet Paul Choudary, <em>Platform Revolution</em> (2016). Platform design and governance <code>[V: W. W. Norton, 28 March 2016, ISBN 9780393249132]</code>.</li>\n<li>David S. Evans and Richard Schmalensee, <em>Matchmakers: The New Economics of Multisided Platforms</em> (2016) <code>[V: Harvard Business Review Press, 24 May 2016, ISBN 9781633691728]</code>.</li>\n<li>Jean-Charles Rochet and Jean Tirole, &quot;Platform Competition in Two-Sided Markets,&quot; <em>Journal of the European Economic Association</em> (2003), the academic foundation for pricing on two-sided platforms <code>[V: Journal of the European Economic Association 1(4), 990 to 1029, June 2003, doi 10.1162/154247603322493212]</code>.</li>\n<li>Andrew Chen, <em>The Cold Start Problem</em> (2021), on launching networks <code>[V: Harper Business, 7 December 2021, ISBN 9780062969743]</code>.</li>\n<li>Apple, <em>App Review Guidelines</em> (developer.apple.com); Apple's press releases of 14 July 2008 and 18 November 2020 (apple.com/newsroom); Google, &quot;Boosting developer success on Google Play,&quot; Android Developers Blog, 16 March 2021; and Apple's Form 10-Q for the quarter ended June 2026 (SEC). Primary sources for CS-C19-02 <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</li>\n<li>The public documentation and changelogs of major API platforms, as worked examples of developer experience and versioning <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</li>\n<li>Ron Kohavi, Diane Tang and Ya Xu, <em>Trustworthy Online Controlled Experiments</em> (2020), on interference when testing changes in two-sided markets <code>[V: Cambridge University Press, 2 April 2020, ISBN 9781108724265; Twyman's law is chapter 3]</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c20\"></span>\n<h2>C20 — Go-to-Market, Pricing and Growth Basics</h2>\n<span id=\"c20-1-what-interviewers-test\"></span>\n<h3>1. What interviewers test</h3>\n<p>&quot;How would you price this product?&quot; &quot;Design the launch plan for a new feature.&quot; &quot;Our referral program has a viral coefficient of 0.4; what do you do?&quot; &quot;Should we add an ad-supported tier?&quot; &quot;Price a new premium wearable together with its services subscription.&quot; Strategy and product-sense rounds regularly end with go-to-market and pricing, because a product that no one finds, understands or pays for is not a success, and because these questions test whether you can connect a product to the economics of a business. This chapter gives the working depth an associate PM needs: what go-to-market is, how to position and price, how to package, which channels and loops grow a product, how to keep users, how to read unit economics, and how to plan a launch and work with the people who run it.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it shows up.</strong> <code>Inferred</code>: strategy, product and analytical rounds at Google, Meta, Microsoft, Amazon, Apple, Netflix, Stripe and others <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>, usually as a follow-on (&quot;now how would you launch or price it?&quot;) and, for some roles, as a dedicated question. On the job it is the work a PM does with product marketing, sales and finance.</p>\n<p><strong>What is scored.</strong> Whether you tie price, positioning and channel to a specific customer and to the economics <code>Inferred</code>. Interviewers listen for a clear target customer and value; evidence for willingness to pay (not just cost-plus); packaging and the metric you charge on; the right growth mechanism for the product; numbers for acquisition cost, lifetime value and payback; and a launch plan with criteria for success and rollback.</p>\n<p>Six observable behaviors:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You start from the customer, the problem and the value, not from a price.</li>\n<li>You position the product against a real alternative, with a message a customer would repeat.</li>\n<li>You price from willingness to pay and costs, and test it, not from a guess.</li>\n<li>You choose what to package and what to charge on, and say what each tier is for.</li>\n<li>You distinguish a funnel from a loop, and compute what a loop can and cannot do.</li>\n<li>You compute acquisition cost, lifetime value and payback, say what would change them, and plan a launch with success and rollback criteria.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The failure this prevents.</strong> Six patterns end GTM answers: a price with no customer or evidence, cost-plus pricing for a product whose value is far above its cost, a launch plan that is a list of tasks, &quot;go viral&quot; as a strategy, unit economics quoted without assumptions, and a launch with no success measure.</p>\n<span id=\"c20-2-the-concept-from-first-principles\"></span>\n<h3>2. The concept from first principles</h3>\n<span id=\"c20-c20-1-what-go-to-market-is\"></span>\n<h4>C20.1 What go-to-market is</h4>\n<p><strong>Go-to-market</strong> (GTM) is the plan for how a product reaches its target customers and wins them: who the customer is, the problem and value proposition, the positioning and message, the price and packaging, the channels, the launch, and the metrics that show whether it is working (FW-C20-01). It answers four questions: <em>who</em> will buy or use this and why; <em>what</em> do we say to them; <em>how</em> do they find and pay for it; and <em>how will we know</em> it is working.</p>\n<p><strong>Motions.</strong> Common ways of reaching customers <code>Inferred</code>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Product-led.</strong> The product itself acquires, converts and expands users: free or self-serve trials, in-product invitations, viral sharing. Typical for consumer apps and developer tools.</li>\n<li><strong>Sales-led.</strong> Salespeople find and close customers, often with demos, contracts and negotiated prices. Typical for enterprise software.</li>\n<li><strong>Partner-led.</strong> Resellers, integrators, platforms or distribution partners bring the product to their customers.</li>\n<li><strong>Hybrid.</strong> A product-led entry that grows into sales for larger accounts.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Who owns what.</strong> The PM owns the <em>why and what</em> (the customer problem, the value, the product); product marketing usually owns positioning, messaging and launch communications; sales owns relationships and closing; support owns the post-sale experience; finance owns pricing governance and forecasts <code>Inferred</code>. In practice the PM co-owns positioning and pricing decisions and carries the launch, with product marketing as the closest partner (C20.10).</p>\n<p><strong>Consumer and business differ.</strong> A consumer product has many small buyers, little sales contact, price sensitivity and virality; a business product has fewer, larger buyers, buying committees, security and procurement requirements, and longer cycles. The same framework applies; the channels and metrics differ.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> Asked how to launch a new expense-tracking feature, a candidate says: &quot;Build a landing page, run social ads, and send an email to all users.&quot; The interviewer asks who it is for and why they would switch from what they use now. The candidate has listed tactics with no target, no value and no measure. A better start: &quot;The target is small-business owners who track expenses in spreadsheets; the value is that receipts are captured automatically; the alternative is the spreadsheet. Success is the share of active owners who capture ten receipts in their first two weeks.&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>When not to over-plan.</strong> For a small, reversible feature a one-paragraph plan and a staged rollout may be enough (C17); a full GTM plan is for products and features whose adoption depends on persuading customers or on changing how they pay.</p>\n<span id=\"c20-c20-2-positioning-and-messaging\"></span>\n<h4>C20.2 Positioning and messaging</h4>\n<p><strong>Positioning</strong> (defined in C18) is the place a product holds in a customer's mind relative to the alternatives. <strong>Messaging</strong> is how that is said to a specific audience (FW-C20-02). The ladder:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Target:</strong> who, specifically (a segment by need or behavior, not demographics).</li>\n<li><strong>Problem and alternative:</strong> what they struggle with and what they use now.</li>\n<li><strong>Value proposition:</strong> the benefit that matters most to them, in their words.</li>\n<li><strong>Proof:</strong> evidence that it works (a number, a customer, a demonstration).</li>\n<li><strong>Differentiation:</strong> why this and not the alternative, and what you will not claim.</li>\n<li><strong>Message per audience:</strong> the same truth in the words of the buyer, the user and, where different, the approver.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A positioning statement template: <em>For [target] who [problem or need], [product] is a [category] that [key benefit]. Unlike [alternative], it [differentiator].</em> The test of a good statement is that a customer could repeat it, and that a competitor could not truthfully say the same.</p>\n<p><strong>Test messaging</strong> with real customers: show two or three versions and ask which they would repeat to a colleague and what they think the product does; run a landing-page or ad test with the conversion rate (C05) as the measure and a guardrail on the quality of sign-ups. Beware of claims you cannot prove, and of messages that describe features instead of outcomes.</p>\n<span id=\"c20-c20-3-pricing-models-and-willingness-to-pay\"></span>\n<h4>C20.3 Pricing models and willingness to pay</h4>\n<p><strong>Willingness to pay</strong> is the most a customer is prepared to pay for something, which varies by customer and by what they compare it with. Price should sit between a floor set by cost and strategy and a ceiling set by willingness to pay and alternatives (FW-C20-03).</p>\n<p><strong>Three anchors.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Cost (the floor).</em> Cost to serve per unit plus a required margin. Cost-plus pricing sets the price from cost alone; it is a floor, not a method, because it ignores value: a product that saves customers $1,000 a year should not be priced at its $20 cost.</li>\n<li><em>Competitors and substitutes (the reference).</em> What customers compare with, including doing nothing.</li>\n<li><em>Value (the ceiling).</em> The benefit to the customer, in money or time, that they would perceive.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Estimating willingness to pay.</strong> Interviews and observation of what people pay now; price-sensitivity surveys that ask at what price a product becomes too cheap to trust, a bargain, expensive and too expensive (a method often credited to Peter van Westendorp <code>[V: van Westendorp, ESOMAR Congress paper, 1976]</code>); conjoint analysis (customers choose among product profiles with different features and prices, and the analysis infers the value of each feature); and, best, live tests of price on real traffic with a holdout and guardrails (C14). Stated willingness to pay overstates what people actually pay <code>Inferred</code>, so treat survey figures as relative guides and test.</p>\n<p><strong>Pricing models.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Model</th>\n<th>How it works</th>\n<th>Fits</th>\n<th>Risk</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>One-time purchase</td>\n<td>Pay once</td>\n<td>Hardware; simple software</td>\n<td>No recurring revenue; upgrades needed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Subscription</td>\n<td>A recurring fee for access</td>\n<td>Services, software, content</td>\n<td>Churn; value must recur</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Usage-based</td>\n<td>Pay for what you use</td>\n<td>Infrastructure, APIs, AI features</td>\n<td>Unpredictable bills for customers (the upside: revenue tracks cost)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tiered</td>\n<td>Several plans at different prices and features</td>\n<td>Mixed customer needs</td>\n<td>Complexity; wrong fences</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Freemium</td>\n<td>Free tier, paid upgrade</td>\n<td>Products with low marginal cost and viral use</td>\n<td>Free users cost money and may never convert</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ad-supported</td>\n<td>Users free, advertisers pay</td>\n<td>Large audiences</td>\n<td>Trust, experience, regulation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Marketplace take rate</td>\n<td>A share of each transaction (C19)</td>\n<td>Two-sided markets</td>\n<td>Disintermediation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Premium pricing.</strong> A high price can signal quality and fit a segment that values craft, status or reliability; it needs a product and positioning that justify it and a segment large enough to matter. Test with willingness-to-pay evidence, not with a belief that &quot;expensive means better.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Bundles.</strong> A bundle sells several products for one price. It tends to raise revenue when customers' valuations of the parts differ and are negatively correlated, because the bundle price can fit all of them (Worked example 2). It tends to fail when everyone values the same part, or when the bundle includes things that customers see as filler.</p>\n<p><strong>Services attach.</strong> For a device or a product with a related service (a wearable with a subscription), the economics have two parts: the product's margin and the service's recurring contribution times the <em>attach rate</em> (the share of buyers who subscribe) and their retention (Worked example 1). A trial or an included period can raise attach and costs service delivery; compare the lifetime contributions, not just the first year.</p>\n<p><strong>Usage-based pricing for AI features.</strong> AI features carry real cost per use (C24.7, C25.6), so a flat price can lose money on heavy users. Options: price per seat with an included allowance and overage; price per task or per outcome; or a hybrid. Choose a <strong>value metric</strong> (below) that tracks the value the customer gets and the cost you bear.</p>\n<span id=\"c20-c20-4-packaging\"></span>\n<h4>C20.4 Packaging</h4>\n<p><strong>Packaging</strong> is the decision about what is included in each plan or tier, and how the plans are named and fenced, so that different customers choose the plan that fits them (FW-C20-03). A <strong>value metric</strong> is the unit on which price is charged (seats, tasks, storage, transactions, outcomes), chosen so that the price grows as the customer's value grows and as your cost grows.</p>\n<p><strong>Principles.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Choose the value metric well.</em> A good one is understood by the customer, grows with their success, is measurable and fair, and tracks your cost (a per-seat price for a product whose cost is tasks can lose money on heavy users; a per-task price for a collaboration tool can discourage use).</li>\n<li><em>Good, better, best.</em> Three tiers are common: an entry plan, a main plan most customers should pick, and a premium plan for power or enterprise needs. The middle plan is the one you are designing.</li>\n<li><em>Fences, not walls.</em> Differentiate tiers by what matters to different segments (limits, advanced features, support, security), so customers can see why they would upgrade, and keep the entry plan genuinely useful.</li>\n<li><em>Upgrade triggers.</em> Know what causes a customer to outgrow a plan (a limit reached, a team added, a compliance need), and make the upgrade easy at that moment.</li>\n<li><em>Avoid complexity.</em> Too many plans and add-ons confuse buyers and support; simplicity has value.</li>\n<li><em>Grandfathering and migration.</em> Changing prices or tiers affects existing customers; plan communication and transition (CS-C20-02).</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c20-c20-5-channels-growth-loops-versus-funnels-product-led-growt\"></span>\n<h4>C20.5 Channels, growth loops versus funnels, product-led growth</h4>\n<p><strong>Channels</strong> are the routes by which customers find a product: organic search and content, app stores, paid advertising, referral and word of mouth, partnerships and integrations, email and owned channels, sales and events <code>Inferred</code>. Each has a cost, a volume, a quality and a speed; choose by the customer's behavior and by unit economics (C20.7).</p>\n<p><strong>Funnel versus loop.</strong> A <em>funnel</em> (C05) is a one-way path: awareness, sign-up, activation, purchase; new users must be bought or earned at the top each time. A <strong>growth loop</strong> is a closed system in which the output of one cycle becomes the input of the next, so that growth compounds: for example, a user invites friends, some join, and those new users invite others (FW-C20-05). Loops exist in referral, content (users create content that attracts search traffic and new users), marketplaces (supply attracts demand attracts supply, C19) and data (usage improves the product, which attracts usage).</p>\n<p><strong>Viral coefficient.</strong> For a referral loop, the viral coefficient <em>k</em> is the number of new users each user brings: invitations sent per user times the share of invitations that convert. If k is below 1, each cohort brings fewer users than the last; the total from an initial group of N users, across all generations, is N ÷ (1 − k). If k is 1 or more, growth is self-sustaining in principle, though in practice it fades as the network saturates and quality falls. Cycle time matters as much as k (Worked example 4).</p>\n<p><strong>Product-led growth.</strong> <strong>Product-led growth</strong> is a go-to-market approach in which the product itself drives acquisition, conversion and expansion, typically through a free or self-serve entry, quick time to value and in-product prompts to invite others or upgrade. It works when the product can show value quickly without a salesperson and the cost to serve a free user is low. It struggles when value needs configuration, procurement or training, in which case sales-assisted motion is added.</p>\n<p><strong>Paid channels.</strong> Paid acquisition is scalable and costs money per user; whether it works depends on whether lifetime value exceeds acquisition cost with a margin (C20.7). Use it to test messaging and to scale a proven loop, and watch for diminishing returns as spend grows.</p>\n<span id=\"c20-c20-6-retention-levers\"></span>\n<h4>C20.6 Retention levers</h4>\n<p>Growth is the product of getting users and keeping them; a product that loses most users in the first month wastes every acquisition dollar (C05 on cohorts and retention). Levers <code>Inferred</code>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Activation.</strong> Get the user to the first moment of value quickly: fewer steps, clear defaults, a first success (C05).</li>\n<li><strong>Habit and return triggers.</strong> Give a reason to come back: new content, progress, reminders and notifications used with restraint and respect for the user (C10.10 on dark patterns).</li>\n<li><strong>Depth of value.</strong> Features that increase the product's usefulness over time (stored data, integrations, personalization) and, with them, <strong>switching costs</strong> (C18).</li>\n<li><strong>Community and network effects.</strong> Value from other users (C18, C19).</li>\n<li><strong>Onboarding and education.</strong> Help users reach advanced value; many churned users never found the valuable feature.</li>\n<li><strong>Pricing and plan fit.</strong> Annual plans reduce churn and may lower flexibility; a plan that does not fit usage creates cancellations.</li>\n<li><strong>Win-back and involuntary churn.</strong> Voluntary churn is a decision to leave; involuntary churn is a failed payment. Fixing failed payments (retries, updating cards, notifications) can recover a meaningful share of churn without any product change <code>Inferred</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Diagnose before acting.</strong> Segment churn by cohort, plan, reason (survey and observed behavior) and time since sign-up; then target the biggest reason (C13).</p>\n<span id=\"c20-c20-7-unit-economics-at-apm-depth-acquisition-cost-lifetime-\"></span>\n<h4>C20.7 Unit economics at APM depth: acquisition cost, lifetime value, payback</h4>\n<p><strong>Customer acquisition cost</strong> (CAC) is the average cost to acquire one new customer: the sales and marketing spend in a period divided by the new customers acquired in it. <strong>Lifetime value</strong> (LTV) is the total contribution a customer is expected to generate over their life with the product. <strong>Payback period</strong> is the time it takes for a customer's contribution to repay the cost of acquiring them (FW-C20-06; C18.6 gave the general method).</p>\n<p><strong>Simple formulas.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Contribution per month</em> = average revenue per user (ARPU) × gross margin.</li>\n<li><em>LTV</em> ≈ contribution per month ÷ monthly churn rate (this assumes constant churn; real cohorts rarely behave that way).</li>\n<li><em>Payback period</em> (months) = CAC ÷ contribution per month.</li>\n<li><em>LTV:CAC ratio</em> = LTV ÷ CAC. A common heuristic is that a ratio of about 3 or more and a payback of under about 12 months indicate a healthy business <code>[V: arithmetic checked; the 3:1 and 12-month benchmarks are common heuristics, not sourced]</code>; it varies with the business, and cash constraints change the picture.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Read them with care.</strong> CAC must include all acquisition cost (salaries, tools, promotions), not only ad spend; blended CAC hides channel differences (compute it by channel); LTV depends on churn, which is uncertain and changes by cohort; margins must be after cost to serve (for AI features this is large); and payback matters more than LTV when cash is short. Do sensitivity: what happens to the ratio if churn rises one point or CAC rises twenty percent (Worked example 5).</p>\n<span id=\"c20-c20-8-launch-planning\"></span>\n<h4>C20.8 Launch planning</h4>\n<p>A launch is a coordinated set of actions that makes a product or feature known, available and supported (FW-C20-07; C17 covers rollout and execution decisions).</p>\n<p><strong>Tiers of launch.</strong> <em>Quiet release</em> (rolled out, little announcement) for small changes; <em>beta or early access</em> (a limited audience, for learning); <em>general availability with announcement</em> (marketing, press, enablement) for major products. Choose by the importance of the change and the cost of getting it wrong.</p>\n<p><strong>The checklist.</strong> Positioning and messaging agreed; pricing and packaging set; the product ready (quality, performance, accessibility, privacy review); documentation and help content; support and sales enablement; instrumentation and dashboards (C05); communications plan (in-product, email, announcements); the rollout plan with stages and rollback triggers (C17); and owners and dates.</p>\n<p><strong>A timeline.</strong> About eight weeks out: positioning, pricing and success metrics set; six weeks: beta and feedback; four weeks: documentation, enablement and communications drafted; two weeks: readiness review; launch week: staged rollout, monitoring, a daily check; two to four weeks after: review against success criteria, learnings and adjustments.</p>\n<p><strong>Success criteria.</strong> Decide in advance the primary metric (adoption among the target segment within a time window), guardrails (support contacts, errors, cancellations, performance) and the decision rule (what result leads to expansion, iteration or rollback).</p>\n<span id=\"c20-c20-9-interview-forms\"></span>\n<h4>C20.9 Interview forms</h4>\n<p><strong>&quot;How would you price this?&quot;</strong> Answer structure: (1) <em>Goal</em> of pricing (revenue, share, margin, positioning). (2) <em>Customer and value:</em> who, what the product is worth to them, their alternatives. (3) <em>Anchors:</em> cost floor, competitor reference, value ceiling. (4) <em>Model and packaging:</em> subscription, usage or tiers, the value metric and the tiers. (5) <em>Evidence and test:</em> how you would estimate willingness to pay and test (C14). (6) <em>Metrics and guardrails:</em> conversion, revenue per user, churn, margin. (7) <em>Risks:</em> cannibalization, perception, complexity. State a price and what would change it.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;Launch plan for X.&quot;</strong> Target and value; the positioning; the channels; the launch tier and timeline; readiness checklist; success criteria with guardrails and rollback triggers; owners.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;Go-to-market for a new feature.&quot;</strong> Who it is for within the existing base, how they will find out (in-product first), how it is explained, how it is packaged or priced (included or add-on), and how adoption and retention impact are measured with a holdout.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;Should we add a tier or a benefit?&quot;</strong> Costs and benefits per member, cannibalization of existing tiers, effect on acquisition and retention, and a test.</p>\n<span id=\"c20-c20-10-working-with-product-marketing-sales-and-support\"></span>\n<h4>C20.10 Working with product marketing, sales and support</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Product marketing</strong> needs the value proposition, the target customer and evidence, early access to the product, launch dates and a clear decision on pricing and packaging. They give you customer language, competitive insight and messaging tests. Agree who decides the message and who decides the price.</li>\n<li><strong>Sales</strong> needs an honest roadmap, a clear value story, enablement and a fast route for customer feedback; they give you customer needs and objections. Learn to say no with an alternative (C32.7) to custom requests.</li>\n<li><strong>Support</strong> needs advance notice of changes, documentation, known issues and a path to engineering; they give you the voice of confused and frustrated users at scale.</li>\n<li><strong>Finance</strong> reviews pricing, forecasts and margins; bring numbers and assumptions early.</li>\n<li><strong>Legal and privacy</strong> review claims, terms, consent and regional rules; involve them before the announcement, not after.</li>\n<li><strong>A launch review</strong> with all functions a week before launch, with a readiness checklist and a go/no-go decision, prevents most launch failures.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Regional Lens: India.</strong> Price sensitivity is high in many consumer segments, average revenue per user is often lower than in other markets, and a global price may be unaffordable or may position the product wrongly <code>Inferred</code> <code>[unverified: author's synthesis; no source checked]</code>. Payment methods differ: real-time bank-transfer payments through UPI are widely used for consumer payments, cards are less universal, and cash on delivery remains common in some e-commerce categories; recurring payments are governed by rules for card and UPI mandates, including authentication requirements above stated limits (additional-factor authentication can be waived for recurring payments up to 15,000 rupees, and up to 1,00,000 rupees for mutual-fund subscriptions, insurance premiums and credit card bills), which affect how subscriptions and free-trial conversions are designed <code>[V: RBI circular RBI/2023-24/88 of 12 December 2023; e-mandate framework of August 2019]</code>. Distribution norms include telecom and super-app partnerships, local-language support, shorter pricing periods (weekly, monthly or prepaid plans rather than annual), and mobile-first, low-data experiences (C09) <code>Inferred</code>. The practical consequences for a PM: test local price points, offer payment methods that people actually use, design trials and renewals around mandate rules, and treat the local market as its own GTM, not a translation of the global one.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c20-3-frameworks\"></span>\n<h3>3. Frameworks</h3>\n<p>Seven frameworks, set out below. The metric card (FW-C05-01), the unit-economics sheet (FW-C18-06), the experiment decision-rule table (FW-C14-05), the launch-or-hold table (FW-C17-04), the AI unit-economics sheet (FW-C25-06) and the no script (FW-C32-07) are reused, not repeated.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c20-01\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C20-01 · The go-to-market one-pager · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a product or feature, its target customer and the business goal.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> one page: the target and the problem; the value proposition and positioning; price and packaging; the motion and channels; the launch tier and timeline; success metrics with guardrails and a decision rule; the owners (PM, product marketing, sales, support, finance, legal).</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the target customer can be named and reached.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a one-pager forces choices and leaves detail to supporting documents.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> a list of tactics; no target; no success measure.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 1 and EX-C20-01.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Answer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Target and problem</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Value proposition and positioning</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Price and packaging</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Motion and channels</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Launch tier and timeline</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Success metrics, guardrails, decision rule</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Owners</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c20-02\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C20-02 · The positioning and messaging ladder · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a product and a segment.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> target, problem and alternative, value proposition, proof, differentiation and the message for each audience; a positioning statement; a messaging test plan with a measure.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> you can talk to customers.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a sharp message excludes some customers.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> feature lists; claims without proof; a statement a competitor could also make.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 3 and EX-C20-02.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"fw-c20-03\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C20-03 · The pricing worksheet · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> the product, its customers and costs.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> the pricing goal; the cost floor; the competitor and substitute reference; the value and willingness-to-pay evidence; the model, value metric and tiers; the test plan; metrics and guardrails; risks (cannibalization, perception, complexity); a price and what would change it.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> survey figures overstate real willingness to pay, so the price is tested.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a higher price raises margin and lowers volume; the right point depends on the goal.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> cost-plus only; copying a competitor; no test; a price with no packaging logic.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked examples 1 and 3.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Answer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Goal</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cost floor</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reference (competitors, substitutes)</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Value and willingness to pay</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Model, value metric, tiers</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Test plan</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metrics and guardrails</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risks and what would change the price</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c20-04\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C20-04 · The bundle test · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> customer segments and their valuations of each component.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> revenue and margin under separate pricing (the best price for each component) and under a bundle price; the correlation of valuations; the conclusion (bundle when valuations differ and are negatively correlated); a check for filler components and cannibalization.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> valuations are estimated from research, and marginal cost is known.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> bundles raise revenue when valuations differ and can hide the value of each part.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> bundling when all customers value the same part; assuming bundles always help.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 2 and EX-C20-04.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"fw-c20-05\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C20-05 · The growth-loop map · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a product and its acquisition mechanisms.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> the loop drawn as a cycle (input, action, output that becomes the next input); for a referral loop, invitations per user, conversion and k; the multiplier N ÷ (1 − k); the cycle time; the cumulative users by cycle; the guardrails (quality, spam, trust); and how a funnel feeds the loop.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> invitations and conversion are measured, not guessed.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> incentives raise k and may bring low-quality users.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> &quot;go viral&quot; as a plan; ignoring cycle time; k above 1 assumed to last.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 4 and EX-C20-05.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Formulas: <strong>k = invitations per user × conversion rate; total users from a starting group N (k &lt; 1) = N ÷ (1 − k).</strong></p>\n<span id=\"fw-c20-06\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C20-06 · The acquisition unit-economics sheet · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> ARPU, gross margin, monthly churn, fully loaded acquisition cost by channel.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> contribution per month, LTV, payback period, LTV:CAC ratio, a sensitivity grid (churn and CAC), the break-even churn and CAC, and a decision (scale, hold or fix).</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> churn is constant for the calculation and checked against cohorts.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a simple LTV is easy to compute and sensitive to the churn estimate.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> ad spend only in CAC; blended CAC; margins before cost to serve.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> Worked example 5 and EX-C20-06.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Formulas: <strong>contribution = ARPU × gross margin; LTV ≈ contribution ÷ churn; payback = CAC ÷ contribution.</strong></p>\n<span id=\"fw-c20-07\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C20-07 · The launch plan · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a product or feature, the audience and the date.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> the launch tier; a readiness checklist (positioning, price, product, documentation, enablement, instrumentation, communications, rollout and rollback); a timeline from about eight weeks before to four weeks after; success criteria with guardrails and a decision rule; owners.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> functions meet in a launch review a week before.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> a larger launch gets attention and raises the cost of failure.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> a task list with no success measure; no rollback; support not told.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> EX-C20-07 and IQ-C20-08.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c20-4-answer-architecture\"></span>\n<h3>4. Answer architecture</h3>\n<span id=\"c20-the-how-would-you-price-this-answer-for-20-minutes\"></span>\n<h4>The &quot;How would you price this?&quot; answer, for 20 minutes</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Minutes</th>\n<th>What to produce</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Goal and customer</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>What pricing should achieve; who buys; the problem and alternatives</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Anchors</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Cost floor, competitor reference, value ceiling (with a number each)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Model and packaging</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>The pricing model, the value metric, the tiers and what each is for</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evidence and test</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>How to estimate willingness to pay and test the price with guardrails</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Economics and risks</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Margin, attach or conversion, cannibalization, perception</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Recommendation</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>A price, the packaging and what would change it</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Pacing rules.</strong> If the interviewer gives a specific constraint (a competitor's price, a margin target), build on it. If you run behind, keep the anchors, the model and the recommendation.</p>\n<p><strong>How to say it.</strong> &quot;I'd price between the floor and the ceiling: the cost to serve is about $200 a unit, which with a 55% margin gives a floor near $445; competitors sit at $300 to $500; and the value is in the daily health insight. I'd test $449.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>How to close.</strong> &quot;So $449 with a subscription at $9.99, tested against $399 and $499 on a holdout. I'd change it if conversion falls more than expected or if the service attach rate falls below about 43%.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c20-type-price-a-product-or-feature\"></span>\n<h4>Type: price a product or feature</h4>\n<p>Goal, customer and value, anchors, model and packaging, test, economics, risks, a recommendation.</p>\n<span id=\"c20-type-plan-a-launch\"></span>\n<h4>Type: plan a launch</h4>\n<p>Target and value, positioning, channels, tier and timeline, readiness checklist, success criteria and rollback.</p>\n<span id=\"c20-type-a-growth-mechanism-referral-virality-channel\"></span>\n<h4>Type: a growth mechanism (referral, virality, channel)</h4>\n<p>Funnel versus loop; k and cycle time; guardrails on quality; unit economics by channel.</p>\n<span id=\"c20-type-add-a-tier-benefit-or-bundle\"></span>\n<h4>Type: add a tier, benefit or bundle</h4>\n<p>Costs and benefits per customer; cannibalization; the bundle test; a test with a holdout.</p>\n<span id=\"c20-type-unit-economics-and-cac\"></span>\n<h4>Type: unit economics and CAC</h4>\n<p>The sheet: contribution, LTV, payback, ratio; sensitivity; a decision rule.</p>\n<span id=\"c20-common-interviewer-interventions-and-what-to-do\"></span>\n<h4>Common interviewer interventions, and what to do</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Interviewer says</th>\n<th>What it means</th>\n<th>What to do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;How did you get that price?&quot;</td>\n<td>Evidence</td>\n<td>Floor, reference, value, and a test</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;A competitor charges half.&quot;</td>\n<td>Positioning and value</td>\n<td>Differentiation, segment, willingness to pay</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;What happens to existing customers?&quot;</td>\n<td>Transition</td>\n<td>Grandfathering, communication, migration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;Won't this cannibalize our main plan?&quot;</td>\n<td>Tier logic</td>\n<td>Fences, a test of mix, a guardrail</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;How would you know the launch worked?&quot;</td>\n<td>Success criteria</td>\n<td>A metric, guardrails and a decision rule</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>&quot;That's a big CAC.&quot;</td>\n<td>Economics</td>\n<td>Payback, LTV, sensitivity, channel split</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c20-5-worked-examples\"></span>\n<h3>5. Worked examples</h3>\n<p>All five examples are <code>Illustrative</code>: the products and figures are invented (invented), and the arithmetic was computed. Company types are settings only, with no claim about any real company's prices or economics.</p>\n<span id=\"c20-worked-example-1-pricing-a-premium-wearable-with-its-service\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 1 — Pricing a premium wearable with its services subscription (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><em>Setting: a premium wearable device and a subscription, of the kind several large device companies sell. No fact about any real product or price is claimed.</em></p>\n<p><strong>The situation.</strong> A team plans a premium health wearable and a health-services subscription. The all-in unit cost (parts, assembly, logistics and a warranty allowance) is $200. The target gross margin on the device is 55%.</p>\n<p><strong>The cost floor (FW-C20-03).</strong> Price at a 55% margin: $200 ÷ (1 − 0.55) = about $444. So the price should not be below roughly $445 if the margin target holds.</p>\n<p><strong>Willingness to pay.</strong> A survey of 1,000 target customers (invented) asks whether they would buy at four prices: 62% at $349, 41% at $449, 22% at $549 and 9% at $649. Stated intent overstates purchase, so use it for relative comparison. The margin index (price minus cost, times the share willing) is: at $349, (349 − 200) × 0.62 = 92.4; at $449, 249 × 0.41 = 102.1; at $549, 349 × 0.22 = 76.8; at $649, 449 × 0.09 = 40.4. The highest margin per respondent is at $449, which also clears the floor, and competitors reference prices between about $300 and $500 (invented). Recommended starting price: $449, tested against $399 and $499.</p>\n<p><strong>The services economics.</strong> The subscription costs $9.99 a month (about $120 a year) with a 70% gross margin. Suppose 35% of buyers subscribe in year one and 80% of subscribers renew each year. Revenue per device sold: year one 0.35 × $119.88 = $41.96; year two $33.57 (80% retained); year three $26.85. Three-year services revenue is about $102.4 per device and, at 70% gross margin, contribution is about $71.7 per device. The device itself earns $249 at $449, so services add about 29% to the three-year contribution per device (71.66 ÷ 249).</p>\n<p><strong>A trial that raises attach.</strong> Suppose giving three months free would raise attach from 35% to 50% but cost $3.00 a month to serve ($9.00 per trial user) for the 80% of buyers who activate it ($7.20 per device). Year one: 50% of buyers pay for nine months, $44.95 of revenue, contribution 0.70 × $44.95 − $7.20 = $24.27, lower than the $29.37 without the trial. Years two and three: $33.57 and $26.85 of contribution (50% attach, retained at 80%), against $23.50 and $18.80 without the trial. Three-year contribution with the trial is $84.69, against $71.66 without it: the trial wins by about $13 per device if the 50% attach holds. The break-even attach with the trial is about 43%: below that, the trial loses money. So the decision depends on one assumption, which a test must settle: run the trial for a share of launch buyers and measure attach and retention at 90 days.</p>\n<p><strong>Recommendation.</strong> Launch at $449 with the subscription at $9.99; test the three-month trial on half of buyers; decide on the 90-day attach (continue the trial if attach is at least 45%, drop it if below 43%); watch returns and the services churn as guardrails.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The price came from a floor, a willingness-to-pay curve and the services economics, and the trial decision came down to one testable number.</p>\n<span id=\"c20-worked-example-2-does-a-bundle-help-illustrative\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 2 — Does a bundle help? (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation (FW-C20-04).</strong> Two digital products, A and B, with zero marginal cost. Three customers value them as follows (invented): customer 1 values A at $10 and B at $3; customer 2 values A at $6 and B at $7; customer 3 values A at $3 and B at $10.</p>\n<p><strong>Best separate prices.</strong> Price A at $6: customers 1 and 2 buy, revenue $12 (at $10 only customer 1 buys: $10; at $3 all buy: $9). Price B at $7: customers 2 and 3 buy, revenue $14 (at $10: $10; at $3: $9). Best separate total: $12 + $14 = $26.</p>\n<p><strong>Bundle.</strong> Each customer values the pair at $13 (10 + 3, 6 + 7, 3 + 10). At a bundle price of $13, all three buy: revenue $39. The bundle raises revenue by 50% ($39 against $26).</p>\n<p><strong>Why it works.</strong> The customers' valuations of A and B are negatively correlated: those who value A most value B least. Separate prices lose the low-valuing customer for each product; the bundle price fits all three because the <em>total</em> valuations are equal.</p>\n<p><strong>When it fails.</strong> If everyone valued A highly and B at almost nothing, a bundle with B would add no value and might look like filler; if one customer valued each product at $13 and others at $1, separate prices would do as well. Check the correlation before bundling.</p>\n<p><strong>Other checks.</strong> The marginal cost of components (a costly component can make a bundle unprofitable), cannibalization of single purchases, and how the bundle changes perceived value.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A small table of valuations explains when a bundle helps: when the parts are valued differently across customers.</p>\n<span id=\"c20-worked-example-3-choosing-the-value-metric-illustrative\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 3 — Choosing the value metric (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation (FW-C20-03) (invented).</strong> A software product with an AI feature costs $0.02 per task to serve. Two customers: customer A has 5 users and runs 4,000 tasks a month (a few people using it heavily); customer B has 200 users and runs 3,000 tasks a month (many people using it lightly).</p>\n<p><strong>Three pricing options.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Customer A revenue</th>\n<th>A margin</th>\n<th>Customer B revenue</th>\n<th>B margin</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>$20 per seat per month</td>\n<td>$100</td>\n<td>$20</td>\n<td>$4,000</td>\n<td>$3,940</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>$0.05 per task</td>\n<td>$200</td>\n<td>$120</td>\n<td>$150</td>\n<td>$90</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>$8 per seat plus $0.03 per task</td>\n<td>$160</td>\n<td>$80</td>\n<td>$1,690</td>\n<td>$1,630</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Cost to serve: A 4,000 × $0.02 = $80; B 3,000 × $0.02 = $60.</p>\n<p><strong>Read it.</strong> <em>Per seat</em> is nearly unprofitable for customer A (a $20 margin on $100 of revenue, 20%) and very profitable for B; heavy users of the AI feature are underpriced. <em>Per task</em> matches cost and value for A and gives B only $150: it ignores the value B gets from the whole team having access. <em>Hybrid</em> charges both: A pays $160 (50% margin) and B $1,690.</p>\n<p><strong>Which metric tracks value and cost?</strong> The AI feature's cost is per task; the collaboration value is per seat. A hybrid with an included allowance captures both: for example $10 per seat with 100 tasks per seat included and $0.03 for each extra task. Customer A: seats $50, included 500 tasks, extra 3,500 × $0.03 = $105: $155 (margin $75). Customer B: seats $2,000, included 20,000 tasks, extra none: $2,000 (margin $1,940).</p>\n<p><strong>Decision and risks.</strong> Choose a per-seat price with an included task allowance and a per-task overage. Risks: bill shock for customers near the limit (offer alerts and caps); complexity (explain it on one line); gaming (sharing seats). Test with a sample of customers and watch conversion, upgrade rate and margin by segment (C25.6).</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The right value metric made price follow both value and cost, and a table of two customers exposed what each simple model misses.</p>\n<span id=\"c20-worked-example-4-can-a-referral-loop-carry-growth-illustrati\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 4 — Can a referral loop carry growth? (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation (FW-C20-05) (invented).</strong> A consumer app has a referral feature: each user sends 4 invitations on average, and 12% of invitations convert to new users. Paid acquisition brings 10,000 new users in a month at a cost of $20 each ($200,000).</p>\n<p><strong>Viral coefficient.</strong> k = 4 × 0.12 = 0.48. Each new user brings 0.48 further users.</p>\n<p><strong>What the loop adds.</strong> Total users from the 10,000 paid users across all generations: 10,000 ÷ (1 − 0.48) = 19,231. So the loop nearly doubles each paid cohort (a multiplier of 1.92), and the effective cost per user falls from $20 to $200,000 ÷ 19,231 = $10.40 over the long run. But it takes time: if each generation takes two weeks, the cumulative users after one, two, three and four cycles are about 14,800, 17,100, 18,200 and 18,700; most of the benefit arrives within about two months.</p>\n<p><strong>Can it be self-sustaining?</strong> At k = 0.48, no: without paid acquisition, each cohort shrinks by about half. To reach k = 0.9 the team could raise invitations to 6 and conversion to 15% (6 × 0.15 = 0.90). Then the multiplier is 10 and, over five generations, a cohort of 10,000 grows to about 40,950 users (10,000 × (1 + 0.9 + 0.81 + 0.729 + 0.656)); the long-run total approaches 100,000. But k is hard to raise: invitations beyond a few annoy users, and incentives (a reward for inviting) attract invitations from people who do not use the product.</p>\n<p><strong>Guardrails.</strong> Quality of referred users (activation and retention against paid users), spam complaints and unsubscribes, the cost of rewards per converted user, and fraud (fake accounts earning rewards).</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Do not rely on virality alone: keep paid acquisition for the base and improve the loop where it is cheap (make invitations contextual, with a reason to invite: sharing something the recipient values); test an incentive on a holdout and measure retention of referred users, since an incentive that lifts k and lowers quality can cost more than it saves.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Computing k and the multiplier showed that a loop with k below 1 helps but cannot replace acquisition, and that cycle time and quality matter as much as k.</p>\n<span id=\"c20-worked-example-5-is-the-acquisition-economics-healthy-illust\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 5 — Is the acquisition economics healthy? (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>The situation (FW-C20-06) (invented).</strong> A subscription app: ARPU $8 a month, gross margin 75% after cost to serve, monthly churn 5%, fully loaded CAC $45.</p>\n<p><strong>The sheet.</strong> Contribution per month: $8 × 0.75 = $6.00. LTV ≈ $6.00 ÷ 0.05 = $120. LTV:CAC = 120 ÷ 45 = 2.67. Payback = 45 ÷ 6.00 = 7.5 months.</p>\n<p><strong>Read it.</strong> A payback of 7.5 months is good; an LTV:CAC of 2.67 is below the common heuristic of about 3 <code>[V: arithmetic checked; the 3:1 and 12-month benchmarks are common heuristics, not sourced]</code>. The ratio is sensitive to churn: LTV falls to $100 if churn rises to 6% (ratio 2.22) and rises to $150 if it falls to 4% (ratio 3.33).</p>\n<p><strong>Sensitivity grid.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Monthly churn</th>\n<th>CAC $40</th>\n<th>CAC $45</th>\n<th>CAC $60</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>4%</td>\n<td>3.75</td>\n<td>3.33</td>\n<td>2.50</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5%</td>\n<td>3.00</td>\n<td>2.67</td>\n<td>2.00</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6%</td>\n<td>2.50</td>\n<td>2.22</td>\n<td>1.67</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Payback by CAC: $40 gives 6.7 months, $45 gives 7.5 and $60 gives 10.0.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision rule.</strong> Scale paid acquisition where the ratio is at least 3 and payback under 12 months: at current churn that means a CAC of $40 or less, or churn of 4% with CAC up to $45. Otherwise fix retention first: a one-point churn reduction moves the ratio more than a 10% cut in CAC (from 2.67 to 3.33 against 2.67 to 2.96).</p>\n<p><strong>Caution.</strong> CAC should be by channel (the blended $45 may hide a $25 organic channel and a $90 paid one); churn should be read by cohort (new users churn faster than old ones, so a constant 5% overstates the value of long-lived customers and understates the early losses); and LTV is a forecast, not a fact.</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Three numbers and a sensitivity grid turned &quot;is it healthy?&quot; into a decision: scale where the ratio and payback pass, and fix retention before spending more.</p>\n<span id=\"c20-6-baseline-strong-standout\"></span>\n<h3>6. Baseline, Strong, Standout</h3>\n<p><strong>Prompt:</strong> &quot;How would you price a new subscription for a meal-planning app?&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c20-baseline-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Baseline answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;I'd look at what competitors charge and pick a similar price, maybe a bit lower to attract users, around $5 a month, and offer a free trial.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>What is wrong:</em> copies competitors; no customer, no value, no cost, no test.</p>\n<span id=\"c20-strong-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Strong answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;I'd start with who it's for and what it's worth to them: busy households who spend money on groceries and waste some food. If the app saves a family $20 a month in waste, then $5 to $10 is a fair share of the value. Competitors charge $4 to $8, so I'd position around $6.99 a month with a discount for annual. I'd offer a free tier with limited plans and a 14-day trial of the paid tier, and test two prices with a holdout, measuring conversion to paid and 90-day retention.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c20-standout-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Standout answer</h4>\n<p>Everything in the Strong answer, plus:</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd set the price between a cost floor and the value ceiling. Cost to serve is small, so margin isn't binding; the real constraint is conversion and churn. I'd estimate willingness to pay with a price-sensitivity survey and then test, because stated willingness overstates what people pay. Packaging: free for one weekly plan; paid for unlimited plans, shopping-list sync and nutrition tracking; the value metric is the household, not the plan. I'd check unit economics: at $6.99 with 80% margin and 6% monthly churn, LTV is about $93; with a CAC under $30, the ratio is above 3 and payback is about five months; at $45 CAC, the ratio falls to about 2 and I'd fix retention before buying users. I'd run the test on new sign-ups only, to avoid disrupting existing users, with guardrails on trial-to-paid and refunds. I'd change my view if the cheaper price raised conversion enough to make revenue per visitor higher, which I'd compute from the test.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c20-what-moves-an-answer-up-a-band\"></span>\n<h4>What moves an answer up a band</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>From Baseline to Strong</th>\n<th>From Strong to Standout</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Starts from the customer and the value, not competitors</td>\n<td>Anchors (floor, reference, ceiling) and the value metric</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Proposes a test with a metric</td>\n<td>Computes unit economics and a decision rule</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Offers a tier or trial</td>\n<td>Packaging logic, transition and guardrails</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c20-7-company-lens\"></span>\n<h3>7. Company lens</h3>\n<p>All claims describe what companies say publicly or what candidates commonly report, not insider knowledge. Prices, products and interview formats change <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Google [as of 2026-10].</strong> Google monetizes largely through advertising and also sells subscriptions and cloud services <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Alphabet Form 10-K for 2025]</code>. Candidates commonly report strategy and product questions about free products with paid tiers and ad models <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: reason about who pays (users or advertisers), the free tier's cost, and when a paid tier is justified.</p>\n</blockquote>\n";
